{# narrative_engine_plot_adapt.prompt — a scheme has hit a setback; this
   call decides whether the person behind it finds another way or gives up.
   Receives the JSON object produced by PlotAdapt::Compose:
     mastermind { name }, ambition, scheme,
     history [string], remaining [string] — the steps they had left, which
       is the thing being revised; without it the model re-derives the
       same plan,
     failure { step, outcome, progress, caught }, attempt, max_attempts,
     step_types [{ id, description }],
     bio { summary, personality } — the mastermind's biography, read
       straight from their bio file by CharacterBios. Plain text, already
       rendered; no character-profile decorator is involved.
   NOTE: no fenced blocks and no literal braces anywhere in this file; both
   break the render. See docs/CUSTOM_PROMPTS.md. #}
[ system ]
## Task
A scheme has hit a setback. You decide whether the person behind it finds
another way, or gives up.

Both answers are real answers. A schemer who never gives up is not
persistent, they are a machine — and a plot that grinds through every
possible approach is worse reading than one that ends because the person
behind it decided it was not worth it any more. Concede when a real
person would.

**The scheme does not change.** You are deciding how they get there, not
what they are after.

## The steps you may use

{% for s in step_types %}- `{{ s.id }}` — {{ s.description }}
{% endfor %}

The type is the SHAPE of the work. Alongside it you write one line saying
what the step actually is — who it involves, what it gets, why it comes
where it does.

## Output Format
Return a JSON object. No markdown fences. No prose before or after the
object.

`decision` — either `"revise"` or `"concede"`.

**If `"concede"`**, add:

- `reason` — one sentence, third person, saying what made them stop. This
  is what a reader sees, so it should be about the person, not about the
  plan: "she decided the ledger was not worth what it would cost her"
  rather than "the acquire step failed".

**If `"revise"`**, add:

- `plan` — what they do INSTEAD, in order: as many steps as this new way
  to the scheme actually needs and no more. The last step is the one that
  accomplishes the scheme; do not add a step that merely restates it, and
  do not end on `conceal`. Each step has:
  - `type` — one of the step ids above, spelled exactly.
  - `description` — one short concrete line saying what the step is.
  - `label` — one to three words naming what the step IS, with no proper
    nouns. The caption under it on a chart, read at a glance beside the
    others, so it says the KIND of move rather than repeating the type
    or compressing the description.
  - `agent` — who carries it out, as an object with `query` and `label`.
  - `target` — the ONE PERSON it is done to, as a plain string. Include
    it only when the step acts on a person; most steps do not.

## Describing the people a revised step needs

The plugin searches the biographies of everyone in the world for whoever
best fits what you asked for. It never takes a name on trust, so nothing
you write here can conjure a person who does not exist.

`agent.query` is a sentence describing that person, matched against
their biography. Write it as narrowly as the step actually requires and
no narrower: every word you add rules people out, so a requirement the
step never draws on costs you candidates and buys nothing. Say where
they have to be, what they need access to, who has to trust them — and
do not name skills the step does not exercise.

`agent.label` is one to three words naming that kind of person, with NO
proper nouns of any sort.

`target` is a single string and may be either a description of a kind of
person or the name of a specific one; prefer the description. It is
always a single living person — never an object, a place, a document, an
organisation or a group. If what the step acts on is a thing or a place,
it has no target: put it in the description and leave the key out.

A name on its own is not enough. A person's name appears in the
biography of everyone who knows them just as it does in their own, so a
bare name finds their household and their rivals before it finds them.
When you name somebody, say in the same string who they are and what
they do for a living.

**Changing who does the work is a revision in itself.** If the last
attempt was seen, the answer is often the same step given to somebody
less conspicuous rather than a different step entirely.

**This replaces the steps they had left, so it has to be different from
them.** You are shown what those were. Handing back the same steps in the
same order is not a revision, it is a retry, and it is the most common way
to get this wrong.

A revision should react to what actually went wrong. If they were seen,
deal with the person who saw them, or drop the approach that exposed
them. If it was too slow, take a shorter way — fewer steps, or a blunter
one. Changing who does the work, or what they go after, is as much a
revision as changing the steps.

## Hard Constraints
- Output strict JSON. No markdown code fences. No prose before or after
  the JSON object.
- Every `type` MUST be one of the step ids listed above, spelled exactly.
- Every step MUST carry a non-empty `description`.
- Every step MUST carry a `label` of one to three words containing no
  proper noun.
- Every step MUST carry an `agent` with a non-empty `query` and `label`.
- Every `agent.label` MUST be one to three words and MUST NOT contain a
  proper noun.
- A step MUST carry a `target` only when it acts on exactly one person.
  Omit the key otherwise; do not send an empty string.
- `target` MUST be a plain string, not an object, and MUST NOT name an
  object, a place, a document, an organisation, or a group of people.
- The last step MUST NOT be `conceal`.
[ end system ]

[ user ]
## Whose scheme this is

{{ bio.summary }}
{{ bio.personality }}

**{{ mastermind.name }}** is ultimately after this:

> {{ ambition }}

And this scheme is their move toward it. It does not change:

> {{ scheme }}

## What has happened so far

{% if length(history) > 0 %}
{% for h in history %}- {{ h }}
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
Nothing yet; the first step is the one that went wrong.
{% endif %}

## What they were going to do next

{% if length(remaining) > 0 %}
{% for r in remaining %}- {{ r }}
{% endfor %}
This is the plan that is being thrown out. Whatever you return replaces
it.
{% else %}
Nothing — the step that failed was the last one they had.
{% endif %}

## The setback

**{{ failure.step }}** — {{ failure.outcome }}.

{% if length(failure.progress) > 0 %}They had got about {{ failure.progress }} of the way
through it when it went wrong.{% endif %}

{% if failure.caught %}
They were **seen**. Whatever comes next has to account for somebody now
knowing something they should not, and for that person having a reason to
talk.
{% else %}
It simply did not work in time. Nobody knows it was attempted.
{% endif %}

This is attempt {{ attempt }} of at most {{ max_attempts }}. After that
they stop whether they want to or not, so a plan that needs three more
setbacks to pay off is not a plan.

Decide whether {{ mastermind.name }} finds another way to the same end,
or stops. Answer with the object.
[ end user ]
